.TH VCOPY 1 "November 2026" "vcopy 0.2.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
vcopy \- directly browseable, zero-link, multi-platform backup and sync tool with historical versioning
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B vcopy
[\fIOPTIONS\fR] [\fISOURCES\fR... \fIDEST\fR] [\fICOMMAND\fR]
.br
.B vcopy manual
[\fB\-\-markdown\fR]
.br
.B vcopy
\fB\-\-manual\fR | \fB\-\-manual-markdown\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B vcopy
is a lightweight, zero-dependency CLI backup and filesystem synchronization tool written in pure Rust. It creates directly browseable, uncompressed filesystem mirrors on external drives and non-snapshotting filesystems (exFAT, FAT32, NTFS, ext4, APFS) while preserving a full historical point-in-time change record.
.PP
The binary embeds its complete documentation in both plain text and Markdown formats, enabling standalone operation without external files.
.SH QUICK START & SIMPLE EXAMPLES
.SS 1. Basic Single Directory Backup
Syncs \fI/home/user/documents\fR into \fI/mnt/usb/backup\fR:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
$ vcopy /home/user/documents /mnt/usb/backup
.fi
.RE
.PP
Live mirror files are immediately browseable at \fI/mnt/usb/backup/documents/\fR as standard uncompressed files. Historical snapshots are archived under \fI.version-history/\fR.
.SS 2. Multiple Sources
Pass multiple sources positionally or with the \fB\-s\fR / \fB\-\-sources\fR flag:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
# Positional syntax (destination is the final argument)
$ vcopy /home/user/docs /home/user/photos /home/user/music /mnt/usb/backup

# Explicit -s / --sources and -d / --dest flags
$ vcopy -s /home/user/docs -s /home/user/photos -d /mnt/usb/backup

# Read paths from a list file, glob patterns, or stdin ('-')
$ vcopy -f project_paths.txt -d /mnt/usb/backup
$ vcopy -f "backups_*.txt" -d /mnt/usb/backup
$ find /home/user/code -name "*.rs" | vcopy -f - -d /mnt/usb/backup
.fi
.RE
.SS 3. Numbered Versions in a Flat Directory (--flatten)
Store all files in a single flat destination directory without nested folders. Historical revisions receive monotonic numbered version suffixes (\fI.v0\fR, \fI.v1\fR, ...):
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
$ vcopy --flatten /home/user/docs /home/user/photos /mnt/usb/backup
.fi
.RE
.PP
Destination layout with numbered versions:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
/mnt/usb/backup/
|-- report.pdf                     # Current live version (latest)
|-- report.v0.pdf                  # Oldest historical version (v0)
|-- report.v1.pdf                  # Second historical version (v1)
|-- photo.jpg                      # Current live photo
|-- photo.v0.jpg                   # Prior version of photo
|-- notes_from_docs.txt            # Stem collision disambiguated
|-- notes_from_photos.txt          # Stem collision disambiguated
|-- notes_from_photos.v0.txt       # Historical version of notes
|-- .version-history.lock          # Advisory concurrency lock
|-- .version-history.baseline.json # Live state manifest & checksums
`-- .version-history.manifest.jsonl# Append-only snapshot ledger
.fi
.RE
.SS 4. Zero-Obfuscation Configuration Files (-c / --config)
A configuration file uses the \fBexact same command-line flags and parameters\fR:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
$ vcopy -c backup.conf
.fi
.RE
.PP
Direct CLI arguments format (\fIbackup.conf\fR):
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
# backup.conf - exact same flags as the command line!
--sources /home/user/documents
--sources /home/user/photos
--dest /mnt/usb/backup
--flatten
--preserve-permissions
--keep-daily 7
--keep-weekly 4
.fi
.RE
.SS 5. Listing Snapshots
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
$ vcopy list /mnt/usb/backup
.fi
.RE
.SS 6. Point-in-Time Restore
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
# Restore latest mirror state
$ vcopy restore /mnt/usb/backup /tmp/restored

# Restore files as of a specific snapshot timestamp
$ vcopy restore --target-time 2026-11-09_083000 /mnt/usb/backup /tmp/restored
.fi
.RE
.SS 7. Retention & Snapshot Pruning
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
# Retain newest 5 snapshots
$ vcopy prune -n 5 /mnt/usb/backup

# Retain 24 hourly, 7 daily, 4 weekly, 12 monthly, 1 yearly
$ vcopy prune --keep-hourly 24 --keep-daily 7 --keep-weekly 4 /mnt/usb/backup

# Dry run: preview actions without modifying files
$ vcopy prune -n 5 --dry-run -v /mnt/usb/backup
.fi
.RE
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-sources " " \fIPATH\fR
Explicit source directory or file. Can be specified multiple times or comma-separated. Alias: \fB\-\-source\fR.
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-dest " " \fIPATH\fR
Destination backup directory. Overrides positional destination. Alias: \fB\-\-destination\fR.
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-paths-from " " \fIPATTERN_OR_STDIN\fR
Read source paths from a file, glob pattern, or stdin (\fB\-\fR). Can be specified multiple times. Aliases: \fB\-\-files-from\fR, \fB\-\-sources-from\fR.
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-config " " \fIFILE\fR
Path to configuration file (direct CLI arguments format, key-value, TOML, or JSON).
.TP
.BR \-\-flatten
Single-directory flat mode: stores all live and historical files in root using \fI.v0\fR, \fI.v1\fR version numbers.
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-preserve-permissions
Record POSIX file modes (Unix) and readonly attributes (Windows) in manifests and restore them.
.TP
.BR \-\-dry-run
Perform a trial run without making any filesystem modifications.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Print detailed information about copied, modified, and deleted files.
.TP
.BR \-\-target-time " " \fITIMESTAMP\fR
Snapshot timestamp (\fIYYYY-MM-DD_HHMMSS\fR or prefix) or \fIlatest\fR for restore.
.TP
.BR \-\-manual ", " \-\-man
Display the comprehensive user manual in plain text / ASCII man-page format.
.TP
.BR \-\-manual-markdown ", " \-\-manual-md ", " \-\-markdown
Display the comprehensive user manual in Markdown format.
.SH RETENTION POLICY OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-max-versions " " \fIN\fR
Retain only the newest \fIN\fR snapshots (overrides GFS retention).
.TP
.BR \-\-keep-hourly " " \fIN\fR
Number of hourly snapshot buckets to retain (default: 24).
.TP
.BR \-\-keep-daily " " \fIN\fR
Number of daily snapshot buckets to retain (default: 7).
.TP
.BR \-\-keep-weekly " " \fIN\fR
Number of weekly snapshot buckets to retain (default: 4).
.TP
.BR \-\-keep-monthly " " \fIN\fR
Number of monthly snapshot buckets to retain (default: 12).
.TP
.BR \-\-keep-yearly " " \fIN\fR
Number of yearly snapshot buckets to retain (default: 1).
.SH SUBCOMMANDS
.TP
.B auto
(Default) Synchronize sources and automatically prune old snapshots.
.TP
.B create
Create a backup snapshot without pruning.
.TP
.B restore
Restore files to a target directory from a snapshot.
.TP
.B list
List all snapshots in destination with summary metrics.
.TP
.B prune
Manually prune snapshots according to retention rules.
.TP
.B manual
Display the user manual. Use \fB\-\-markdown\fR for Markdown format.
